.\" tafas(1) — manual page. Kept in the repository and installed by the .deb, the
.\" .rpm, the Homebrew formula and the release archives. `tests/packaging.rs`
.\" fails when an option in `tafas --help` is missing here, so this page cannot
.\" drift into describing a different program.
.TH TAFAS 1 "2026-11-04" "tafas 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tafas \- capture a video from YouTube, TikTok, X, Vimeo, Reddit, SoundCloud or Twitch as one playable MP4
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tafas
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIURL\fR...
.br
.B tafas
\fB\-\-list\-formats\fR | \fB\-\-capabilities\fR | \fB\-\-info\fR |
\fB\-\-completions\fR \fI SHELL\fR | \fB\-\-man\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tafas
reads a page, picks a stream, downloads it and writes one file. When a site
serves picture and sound separately, as YouTube does above 720p,
.B tafas
joins them into one MP4 without re-encoding: the sample tables are copied and
the container index is rebuilt, so the output holds the frames the site served.
Nothing is transcoded, no quality setting exists, and no external program is
run.
.PP
Every URL is one item. Items are independent: one failure does not stop the
others, and the exit code says whether any failed. The written path goes to
stdout, one per line; progress, notes, warnings and errors go to stderr. A
closed stdout pipe ends the run quietly and successfully.
.PP
A file is written through a temporary
.I <target>.part
beside the destination and renamed into place only after its byte count has been
checked \- and, for a remuxed file, after its structure has been re-read from
disk. An interrupted run leaves the
.I .part
file behind; the next run continues it when the server supports range requests.
.SH SUPPORTED SITES
.TP
.B YouTube
Watch pages, \fByoutu.be\fR short links, \fB/shorts/\fR, \fB/embed/\fR and
\fB/live/\fR URLs. The watch page's inlined player response is read first; when
that yields nothing, the InnerTube JSON API is asked through a fixed chain of
clients (\fBandroid_vr\fR, \fBios\fR, \fBandroid\fR, the embedded TV client,
\fBweb\fR). Formats that arrive signature-protected are dropped rather than
deciphered, and a page that demands a sign-in check is a refusal.
.TP
.B TikTok
Video and photo posts, including \fBvm.tiktok.com\fR and \fBvt.tiktok.com\fR
short links. The page's rehydration document is read first, the mobile feed
second. The version without a watermark is preferred when the page offers both.
.TP
.B X
Posts with video, animated GIF or photos, through the syndication endpoint. A
post that offers only an HLS playlist is fetched segment by segment, demuxed and
written as an MP4 in process; a progressive MP4 is preferred when both exist.
.TP
.B Vimeo
\fBvimeo.com/<id>\fR, channel URLs, unlisted \fB<id>/<hash>\fR URLs and
\fBplayer.vimeo.com/video/<id>\fR, read from the player's own configuration
endpoint. Progressive MP4s are preferred; a video that offers only an HLS
playlist is fetched, demuxed and written in process. DASH is not read and the
refusal says so, and an unlisted video without its hash is the same 404 a deleted
one gets, which the refusal also says.
.TP
.B Reddit
Posts whose `secure_media.reddit_video` lists a playlist, and bare
\fBv.redd.it/<hash>\fR URLs, read from \fB/comments/<id>.json\fR. Only the HLS
playlist is taken: the progressive \fBDASH_<height>.mp4\fR files carry no audio
at all, and the DASH manifest is `SegmentBase`, which is not read \- both are
refusals that say so rather than a silent file. An over\-18 post with nothing
playable names \fB\-\-cookies\fR.
.TP
.B SoundCloud
Track URLs (\fBsoundcloud.com/<artist>/<track>\fR) and \fBon.soundcloud.com\fR
short links, read from the page's own hydration document. The track is written as
an \fBMP3\fR, copied byte for byte from the CDN address the site's transcoding
names. A set, a Go+ track, an opus-only rendition and a consent wall are refusals
that name themselves; a set is a list, and one URL is one file.
.TP
.B Twitch
\fBtwitch.tv/videos/<id>\fR, through one batched GraphQL request: the playback
token, and the title, length and poster that name the file. The playlist the
token unlocks is assembled into an MP4 like any other HLS list. A channel, a clip
and a subscriber\-only recording are refusals that name themselves; the client id
is public and is not a credential.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Destination
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIPATH\fR
Output file, or directory when more than one URL is given. A trailing slash
forces the directory reading. \fB\-\fR writes a single stream to stdout, which
is refused for anything that needs a remux.
.TP
.B \-\-name \fINAME\fR
Use this name stem instead of \fI<title> [<id>]\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace an existing file. Without it an existing destination is an error, and
nothing is fetched.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-info\-json
Write \fI<name>.info.json\fR beside each file: the page, the title, the streams
the page offered, which one was taken, and the files that were written. Media
URLs are deliberately left out \— a signed URL is a credential that expires
within hours, and a file that outlives it should not carry one.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-thumbnail
Write the page's poster frame beside each file, as \fI<name>.jpg\fR,
\fI<name>.webp\fR or \fI<name>.png\fR: the site's own format, copied byte for
byte and never re\-encoded. It lands after the media file, through a temporary
and a rename. A page that offers no poster says so rather than writing nothing
quietly, and a photo post is skipped by name: every image is already a file.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-subs
Write the page's caption tracks beside each file, as WebVTT:
\fI<name>.<language>.vtt\fR, or \fI<name>.<language>.auto.vtt\fR when the site
transcribed the audio instead of a person writing the cues. One file per track;
nothing is merged. YouTube is the only site of the seven that lists tracks, so
elsewhere this is a note rather than a file.
.TP
.B \-\-embed\-subs
Put one caption track inside the MP4 instead of beside it, as a \fBwvtt\fR
track (WebVTT in ISO\-BMFF). The cues keep their times and their text; cue
settings, \fBSTYLE\fR and \fBREGION\fR blocks are dropped, because the
container's cue box has nowhere to put them and the player places the cue
anyway. The track's language is not carried: \fBmdhd\fR says \fBund\fR, and
the language lives in the sidecar's name. The file is read back, given the
track and laid out again; samples are copied, so nothing is re\-encoded.
One track per file, so the selection has to name one language, and a run that
did not produce exactly one MP4 says so and adds nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-sub\-langs \fILANGS\fR
Which caption languages to write: \fBall\fR (the default), an exact code such
as \fBen\fR or \fBpt\-BR\fR, or a language ending in \fB*\fR such as
\fBen*\fR for \fBen\fR and its variants \fBen\-US\fR and \fBen\-GB\fR \-
but not \fBeng\fR, which is another language. A list selects the union and ignores case. A
selection that matches no track is a refusal naming the languages the page does
have; using this flag without \fB\-\-write\-subs\fR or \fB\-\-embed\-subs\fR
is a usage error, and so is asking for both writers at once.
.SS Selection
.TP
.B \-Q, \-\-quality \fIQUALITY\fR
\fBbest\fR (default), \fBworst\fR, or a height such as \fB720p\fR. A height is a
ceiling: when nothing that small exists, the smallest available stream is taken.
.TP
.B \-\-audio\-only
Take the audio stream alone, as \fBm4a\fR when the site offers one.
.TP
.B \-\-format \fIID\fR
Fetch exactly this stream, using an id printed by \fB\-\-list\-formats\fR.
Requires one URL.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mux
Keep picture and sound in separate files; never remux.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-formats
Print every stream of each URL and stop. Nothing is downloaded.
.SS Transfer
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resume
Do not continue an interrupted download. By default a \fI.part\fR file is
continued with a range request.
.TP
.B \-\-retries \fIN\fR
Retries for one failed request (default 3). Transport errors, \fB429\fR and
\fB5xx\fR are retried with a growing delay; other \fB4xx\fR responses are not.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fISECS\fR
Deadline for one metadata request (default 60). A media transfer has no total
deadline.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-jobs \fIN\fR
How many URLs to work on at once (default 1; \fB0\fR means four).
.TP
.B \-\-concurrent\-fragments \fIN\fR
How many segments of one HLS list to fetch at once, \fB1\fR to \fB16\fR
(default 1, which fetches them one after another). The segments are written in
playlist order whatever order they arrive in, and the first failure stops the
ones still queued. It applies to a segment list only; on any other selection it
prints a note and changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-limit\-rate \fIRATE\fR
Cap a transfer at
.I RATE
bytes per second, written with an optional
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
suffix in powers of 1024 (500K, 2M, 1.5M). One budget is shared by every body
and every segment in flight, so
.B \-\-concurrent\-fragments
divides the rate rather than multiplying it. Below 1K the run is refused rather
than clamped; metadata requests are not charged. The default is no limit.
.TP
.B \-\-cookies \fIFILE\fR
Send the cookies in a Netscape cookie file, in the format curl writes and every
browser exports. The file is read once at startup, sent only to the hosts its
domains match, never written back and never logged. It is the only credential
this program accepts, and it stores nothing between runs.
.TP
.B \-\-extractor\-args \fISITE:KEY=VALUE\fR
Narrow one extractor's route by hand, for the day a site reorders the document
the extractor reads. The vocabulary is closed and checked before the first
request: \fByoutube:client=\fR (\fBandroid_vr\fR, \fBios\fR, \fBandroid\fR,
\fBtv_embedded\fR, \fBweb\fR) pins one InnerTube client and drops the others
from the chain, and \fBtiktok:route=\fR (\fBweb\fR, \fBapp\fR) drops one of
TikTok's two routes. Repeat the flag once per site; naming the same key twice is
a usage error, not a silent last\-one\-wins, and a key or value this build does
not know is refused with the accepted spellings in the message.
.TP
.B \-\-playlist\-items \fIITEMS\fR
Take only these entries of a list: \fB3\fR, \fB1\-5\fR, \fB2\-\fR or any
comma\-separated mix, counted from one. There are two kinds of list. In the
segment list of an HLS playlist the item is a segment, and the file is assembled
from the ones named. In a page that names other videos the item is a video, and
every chosen entry is captured on its own into a directory as
\fB03 \- Title [id].mp4\fR; a dead entry is reported and the rest are still
captured. Three sites have such a page: a YouTube playlist or channel
(\fB/playlist?list=\fR, \fB/@handle\fR, \fB/channel/UC...\fR), a Vimeo album or
showcase (\fB/album/<id>\fR, \fB/showcase/<id>\fR) and a Twitch collection
(\fB/collections/<id>\fR). One level is followed: a list of lists is refused. A selection that matches nothing is a refusal, not an empty file, and
one that does not begin on a keyframe produces a warning, because the first
frames are then undecodable.
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be fetched, in which shape, and where, then stop.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One JSON object per item on stdout. The schema is described in \fIdocs/cli.md\fR.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Errors only. The written path is still printed to stdout, because it is data.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Never draw the progress line.
.TP
.B \-\-color \fIWHEN\fR
\fBauto\fR (default), \fBalways\fR, \fBnever\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Same as \fB\-\-color never\fR.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Log requests and decisions on stderr. Repeatable.
.SS Informational
.TP
.B \-\-completions \fISHELL\fR
Print a completion script for bash, zsh, fish, powershell or elvish, generated
from the live parser.
.TP
.B \-\-man
Print this manual page in groff source and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-capabilities
Print a JSON description of what this build can do. Read-only: it makes no
request and touches no file.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-info
Print the version, the supported sites and the standing defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every URL succeeded, or the answer was informational.
.TP
.B 1
At least one URL failed; the others were still processed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage. Nothing was requested.
.TP
.B 3
A fatal error: the run could not be completed.
.TP
.B 4
A written file failed verification. This is a bug in \fBtafas\fR.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. A \fI.part\fR file may remain; the next run continues it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR, CLICOLOR, CLICOLOR_FORCE, TERM
Decide whether colour is used when \fB\-\-color auto\fR is in effect, in that
order of precedence. \fBTERM=dumb\fR disables colour.
.TP
.B HTTPS_PROXY, HTTP_PROXY, NO_PROXY
Honoured by the HTTP client.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <target>.part
A transfer in progress. Removed by a successful run.
.TP
.I <target>.video.part, <target>.audio.part
The two halves of a remux, present only while it is running or after a failure.
.TP
.I <target stem>.info.json
Written only with \fB\-\-write\-info\-json\fR, and only after the file it
describes has been renamed into place.
.TP
.I <target stem>.jpg, .webp, .png
The poster frame, written only with \fB\-\-write\-thumbnail\fR.
.TP
.I <target stem>.<language>.vtt, <target stem>.<language>.auto.vtt
WebVTT captions, written only with \fB\-\-write\-subs\fR. The same cues can go
inside the file instead, with \fB\-\-embed\-subs\fR.
.TP
.I <target>.part.segments, <target>.part.journal
The segments of an HLS list fetched so far, kept beside the part so an
interrupted run asks only for what is missing. Deleted when the file is
renamed into place, and by \fB\-\-no\-resume\fR.
.PP
On Windows a path past 260 characters is written in its verbatim form (a
prefix in front of it), which lifts the limit and lets a deep output
directory work; nothing about this is visible elsewhere.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.SH BUGS
Signature-protected YouTube formats, HEVC and AC-3 in a fragment, and
\fBSAMPLE-AES\fR are refused rather than worked around; \fIdocs/extractors.md\fR
and \fIdocs/hls.md\fR state exactly what is and is not supported. Report bugs at https://github.com/RUdolf1517/tafas/issues.
